<!--Post: 13350--><!--Time: 1115132689-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13350"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 03, 2005 - 8:04 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I&#39;d so love to see that smackdown. Just sentence Denny and then put him in a locked room with Charlie, who will have full immunity from prosecution. <BR> <BR>Okay, I had to get that neanderthal solution off my otherwise left-leaning chest. <BR> <BR>That said... anyone else find it interesting that Denny didn&#39;t actually speak himself, forcing the judge to enter the automatic not guilty plea? <BR> <BR>Also, Rader rumor mill; Paula may have written him a letter begging him to spare the family the grief of a trial. He might have thought the best he could do was stand mute in that case, but that would show he has regard for someone, and I&#39;m not sure I think he does.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13350-->
<!--Post: 13351--><!--Time: 1115133079-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13351"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 03, 2005 - 8:11 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->To clarify; the title of the thread is a quote from Otero as the arraignment was ending this morning. He called this across the courtroom to Denny, who the reporter was sure heard him. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13351-->
<!--Post: 13352--><!--Time: 1115133193-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13352"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Elsewhere<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Elsewhere<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 03, 2005 - 8:13 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve - I don&#39;t think he has regard for anyone. Only his precious little man self.  I thought it odd also that the ass stood mute and let the judge do the talking.  I heard the rumor also, I think I saw it on Kansas.com discussion or CJ - about Paula begging him to spare the family a trial and to plead guilty - maybe the public defenders talked him into pleading not guilty.  But then, I can&#39;t see anyone being able to talk Rader into anything. <BR>This will be interesting. <BR>He just wants to be in the limelight.... <BR>I like your idea of Denny and Charlie being in a locked room together...how enjoyable that would be.   <BR>I have a funny feeling DTDDC isn&#39;t going to last long... <BR>something will happen. <BR>Security or no Security.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13352-->
<!--Post: 13355--><!--Time: 1115133964-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13355"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 03, 2005 - 8:26 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->What a piece of garbage! I knew I was being overly optomistic to think this sick excuse for a human being would spare the families a trial. We are suppose to pray for souls like his, all be damned if I&#39;m going to. <BR> <BR>Your right Charlie, he won&#39;t last! There are people in different prisons throughout the country that are hoping he is coming their way. You&#39;ll get yours you piece of garbage.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13355-->
<!--Post: 13361--><!--Time: 1115141387-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13361"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Foxfire<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Foxfire<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 03, 2005 - 10:29 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I suppose DTDDC figures he has nothing to lose by pleading not guilty.  He&#39;s in prison now.  He cannot  receive the death penalty.  So the worst that could happen is that he will be found guilty and remain in prison for the rest of his life.  The best is he&#39;ll walk free.  However, so many people despise him that I wouldn&#39;t be surprised if somebody killed him on the outside if he is let go.  He&#39;d have to move to a 3rd world country to escape detection and maybe not even then because so many people all over the world have heard his story now.  He has received the notariety he always craved.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
